# Hub submodule pins (mother repo)

The hub (`new-project-idea`) is a **binder**: each product under `Wolvrine-Solution/<name>` is a **git submodule** pinned to a specific commit. Fresh clones and `bun run sync` only see what the hub records — not “latest on GitHub” unless the pin was updated.

## Hard rules (agents)

1. **Every org product that lives as a hub submodule** — when you push new commits to that product’s default branch (`main` or `master`), **update the hub pointer in the same session** before you consider the task done.
2. **Order:** product repo — commit → push → hub — `bun run bump-pins -- --only <path>` (or `git submodule update --remote --merge -- <path>`) → `git add <path>` → hub commit → **push hub `main`** immediately. Do not leave a local-only pin bump.
3. **Dirty submodule exception:** If that submodule still has uncommitted or untracked work, finish or stash it first — do not record a hub pin that hides dirty state.
4. **New product:** `git submodule add` + row in [README.md](../../README.md) + entry in [hub/projects.yaml](../../hub/projects.yaml) + initial pin commit on hub (same session as the product repo exists on GitHub).
5. **Working inside a product only:** If the workspace root is a single product repo (not the hub), you cannot bump the pin there — note in your handoff that the hub needs `bun run bump-pins -- --only <name>` after merge.

## Commands

```bash
# See stale pins (no writes)
git diff --submodule
bun run status

# Bump one product after its push
bun run bump-pins -- --only wolverine-solution-landing-design
git add wolverine-solution-landing-design
git commit -m "chore: bump wolverine-solution-landing-design submodule"
git push origin main
```

